topics.description:
Conditional statements let a program take decisions.

### if / else if / else
```js
if (temperature >= 30) { /* beach */ }
else if (temperature >= 20) { /* tv */ }
else { /* sleep */ }
```
The condition is converted to a boolean, so any truthy value enters the block. Use early `return` to avoid deep nesting.

### switch
Compares one value against many `case` values using **strict equality** (`===`).
```js
switch (day) {
  case "Monday":
    console.log("work");
    break;
  case "Saturday":
  case "Sunday":     // fall-through groups cases
    console.log("weekend");
    break;
  default:
    console.log("normal day");
}
```
Without `break`, execution **falls through** to the next case. Use `{}` around a case body if you declare `let/const` inside.

### Ternary operator
`condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse` is an expression (it returns a value), ideal for small choices:
```js
const result = score >= 60 ? "Pass" : "Fail";
```
Avoid nesting more than one level.

### Lookup object alternative
For many value-to-value mappings, an object or Map is shorter and faster to read than a long switch.

