topics.description:
A **constructor function** is a normal function, named with a capital letter by convention, designed to be called with `new`.

```js
function Person(name, age) {
  this.name = name;
  this.age = age;
}
Person.prototype.greet = function () {
  return `Hi, I am ${this.name}`;
};
const p = new Person("Asha", 25);
```

### What `new` does
1. Creates a new empty object.
2. Links its prototype to `Person.prototype`.
3. Calls `Person` with `this` set to the new object.
4. Returns the object (unless the constructor returns another object).

### Instance vs prototype members
- Properties set on `this` belong to **each instance** (own properties).
- Methods placed on `Person.prototype` are **shared**, so only one copy exists.

### Checks
```js
p instanceof Person;        // true
p.constructor === Person;   // true
```

### Forgetting `new`
Without `new`, `this` is `undefined` (strict) or `window`, so properties leak to the global object. Guard with `if (!new.target) throw ...`, or use a class, which throws automatically.

### Factory functions
A function that returns a new object without `new`; works well with closures for private data:
```js
const createUser = (name) => ({ name, hello: () => `hi ${name}` });
```

### Built-in constructors
`Date`, `Map`, `Set`, `Promise`, `Error` are used with `new`. Avoid `new String()`, `new Number()` and `new Boolean()` - they create objects, not primitives.

examples.code:
function Person(name, age) {
  this.name = name;
  this.age = age;
}
Person.prototype.greet = function () {
  return `Hi, I am ${this.name} (${this.age})`;
};

const p1 = new Person("Asha", 25);
const p2 = new Person("Ravi", 30);
console.log(p1.greet(), p2.greet());
console.log(p1.greet === p2.greet);            // shared method
console.log(p1 instanceof Person, p1.constructor === Person);
console.log(Object.keys(p1));

function myNew(Ctor, ...args) {
  const obj = Object.create(Ctor.prototype);
  const result = Ctor.apply(obj, args);
  return result instanceof Object ? result : obj;
}
console.log(myNew(Person, "Meera", 22).greet());

