topics.description:
Observer APIs notify you **asynchronously** when something changes, without expensive polling.

### IntersectionObserver
Tells you when an element enters or leaves the viewport (or another container).

```js
const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries, obs) => {
  entries.forEach((entry) => {
    if (entry.isIntersecting) {
      entry.target.classList.add("visible");
      obs.unobserve(entry.target);        // stop watching once done
    }
  });
}, { threshold: 0.3, rootMargin: "0px" });
observer.observe(element);
```
Use cases: **lazy-loading images**, **infinite scroll**, scroll-reveal animations, ad impressions, active menu highlighting. It replaces scroll handlers with `getBoundingClientRect`, which cause layout thrashing.

Options: `root`, `rootMargin`, `threshold` (0 to 1 or an array).

### MutationObserver
Watches **changes to the DOM tree**: children added/removed, attribute or text changes.

```js
const mo = new MutationObserver((mutations) => {
  mutations.forEach((m) => console.log(m.type, m.addedNodes));
});
mo.observe(target, { childList: true, attributes: true, subtree: true });
mo.disconnect();
```
Use cases: reacting to third-party widgets, detecting dynamically inserted elements, building extensions, auto-resizing.

### Related observers
`ResizeObserver` (element size changes) and `PerformanceObserver`.

### Notes
- Callbacks are batched and run asynchronously (MutationObserver as a microtask).
- Call `disconnect()` / `unobserve()` to avoid leaks.

