topics.description:
A `catch` block catches **everything** thrown inside `try` - programmer bugs, network failures, your own validation errors. Good error handling therefore *discriminates*: handle what you expect, rethrow the rest.

### Four ways to identify an error
| Technique | Example | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `instanceof` | `e instanceof TypeError` | best for classes in the **same realm** |
| `name` | `e.name === "AbortError"` | needed for `DOMException`; works across iframes |
| custom `code` field | `e.code === "ENOENT"` | Node.js style, stable machine-readable id |
| `Error.isError(e)` | true for real errors | new (ES2026); works across realms and for DOMException |

### The golden pattern
```js
try {
  doWork();
} catch (e) {
  if (e instanceof ValidationError) {
    showMessage(e.message);        // expected
  } else if (e instanceof NetworkError) {
    scheduleRetry();               // expected
  } else {
    throw e;                       // unexpected: do NOT swallow
  }
}
```

### Cross-realm trap
An error created inside an `iframe` or `vm` context has *that realm's* `Error` constructor, so `e instanceof Error` is **false** in the parent page. Use `Error.isError(e)`, or compare `Object.prototype.toString.call(e) === "[object Error]"`, or check `e.name`.

### Also remember
- Anything can be thrown: `e` might be a string, `null` or a plain object. Normalise first: `const err = e instanceof Error ? e : new Error(String(e))`.
- In TypeScript the caught variable is `unknown` (with `useUnknownInCatchVariables`), forcing a type check.
- Match on **name/instanceof/code**, never on `message` text - it can change between versions and languages.

